=== ANOMALY 1: requested-duration overrun (60s planned, ~88s actual) ===

The plan specifies "Requested run duration: 60 s total, including startup". The
actual observed duration, confirmed independently by THREE sources, was ~88s, not
60s:
  - instr_dump: first_sample_tick=7239, last_sample_tick=16028, sample_span_ticks=8789,
    tick_per_second=100 -> 87.89s guest-side accumulator span.
  - instr_trace: first trace row tick=7239, last trace row tick=16028 (identical
    window, as expected -- same accumulator).
  - host iperf -y C: connection on src_port 49153 produced per-second rows for
    t=0.0 through t=87.0-88.0, and its own summary row is "...,0.0-88.0,13627388,1238575"
    -- an 88-second host-observed interval, matching the guest window almost exactly
    (88.0s host vs 87.89s guest -- consistent to within the measurement granularity).

ROOT CAUSE: this is a control-loop timing defect in my own execution, not a
platform/instrumentation anomaly. I sent `iperf -c` at 20:40:13Z intending to wait
exactly 60s before stopping it, but the actual wait procedure (a background `sleep
62` task plus several intervening tool round-trips while polling for its completion)
consumed additional wall-clock time before I sent `iperf --stop` at 20:41:41Z --
88 seconds after the client started, not 60.

This is NOT a B1/R13-style host-contention stall: hostmon.csv shows load1 in the
0.25-0.28 range throughout (idle 2-vCPU host), and the N8 gate (below) finds the
entire 88s interval series flat with ZERO depressed bins -- i.e. throughput was
uniformly steady for the full 88s, so the extra ~28s is genuinely additional clean
transfer time, not a stall that happened to coincide with a slow stop command.

CONSEQUENCE: this attempt's requested-duration control variable does not match the
plan's "60 s total" specification. It is otherwise a complete, clean, analysis-shaped
run (no gaps, no collapse, no identity ambiguity), and the predeclared [3,60) primary
window is fully contained within the longer available data (ticks exist well past
the 60s mark), so [3,60)/[30,60)/[3,30) sensitivity windows ARE all computable from
this attempt's trace if the coordinator chooses to treat it as usable. But the
deviation itself is flagged here, not silently absorbed -- per CLAUDE.md, run
duration is a controlled variable that must not be silently modified, and per the
plan's own early-stop language this is reported rather than corrected after the
fact. I did NOT attempt to retroactively truncate the trace to make it look like a
60s run.

FOR ATTEMPTS 2-24 (if authorized): recommend timing the 60s window with a single
precise background sleep command whose own completion event is awaited without
intervening diagnostic round-trips, or by polling the host iperf CSV's own
elapsed-time column directly (iperf's own per-second timestamps are already the
ground truth) and stopping as soon as it reports ~60.0s, rather than relying on
wall-clock bookkeeping across many short tool calls.

=== ANOMALY 2: tmux history-limit truncated instr_trace output; fixed via pipe-pane ===

First `instr_trace 1` capture attempt: `tmux capture-pane -p -t pilot20 -S -3000`
(then retried with -S -6000 and -S -20000) returned only ~1866-1867 lines, containing
the INSTR_TRACE_END marker and the tail of the trace (from sample index ~2530 to 4394)
but NOT the INSTR_TRACE_BEGIN marker or the first ~2530 samples. Root cause: the
pilot20 tmux pane's `history-limit` was 2000 (the tmux server default), and the
~4397 lines the trace command printed (plus preceding console output) exceeded that
buffer, so tmux itself discarded the earliest lines in real time as they scrolled
past -- this happened BEFORE any capture-pane call, so it could not be recovered by
capturing more history after the fact.

Attempted fix 1: `tmux set-option -t pilot20 history-limit 20000` then re-ran
`instr_trace 1`. This did NOT work: `tmux list-panes -t pilot20 -F
'#{history_size} #{history_limit}'` continued to report `history-limit 2000` for
the existing pane even after the option was set (tmux 3.4 on this host does not
dynamically resize an already-created pane's scrollback allocation from a
session-level `set-option`, and `-g` did not help either). The second `instr_trace 1`
redump was therefore ALSO truncated, confirmed by an identical ~1866-line capture.

Fix that worked: `tmux pipe-pane -o -t pilot20 "cat >> <scratchpad>/pilot20_full_pane.log"`
started BEFORE the third `instr_trace 1` call. `pipe-pane` tees the pane's raw PTY
output to a file as it is written, independent of the scrollback buffer, so it is
immune to the history-limit truncation. The third capture was complete:
  INSTR_TRACE_BEGIN run_id=pilot20_L0_r50_1 written=4395 retained_from=0 stride=1
  ... 4395 data rows (n=0..4394) ...
  INSTR_TRACE_END run_id=pilot20_L0_r50_1
extracted via `sed -n '/BEGIN.../,/END.../p'` into pilot20_L0_r50_1.trace.
`tmux pipe-pane -t pilot20` (no command argument) was then called to turn piping
back off.

IMPLICATION FOR ATTEMPTS 2-24: start `tmux pipe-pane -o -t pilot20 "cat >> <file>"`
once, immediately after the tmux session is created (before any msh traffic), and
leave it running for the whole block of attempts under one build -- this avoids
needing to repeat the discovery process and guarantees no trace data is ever lost
to scrollback again. This was NOT in the original plan or 07_reproduction_notes.md
and is a new, reusable procedural finding from this session.

