=== instr_dump sanity (full JSON in pilot20_L0_r50_1.json) ===
  run_id: "pilot20_L0_r50_1" -- MATCHES the instr_reset argument (run_id-mismatch
    gate satisfied).
  sample_count=4395, stale_count=0, trace_written=4395, trace_slots=8192 (no wrap,
    well under capacity as predicted).
  gap_min_ticks=2, gap_max_ticks=3 -- sampler mostly hit exactly the requested 2-tick
    (20ms) period, with occasional 3-tick (30ms) gaps (minor scheduling jitter, not
    investigated further here).
  mech_app_limited=137, mech_unsent_pending=2, mech_ack_pending=4256.
    Sum = 137+2+4256 = 4395 = sample_count exactly -- confirms the three mech_*
    fields are exhaustive and mutually exclusive over this run, as the plan's
    Preflight section asserted (reused, not re-derived here).
    As fractions of sample_count: app_limited=3.12%, unsent_pending=0.05%,
    ack_pending=96.84%. Non-trivial, not all-zero, not a degenerate single-category
    result -- ack_pending dominates heavily at L0 (8196-byte buffer)/50ms RTT, which
    is directionally consistent with 08_design_rationale's framing (a small buffer
    at a moderate RTT is expected to spend most samples with everything sent and
    awaiting ACK, not with free buffer space or unsent-but-buffered data) but this
    single attempt does not by itself establish that interpretation formally.
  snd_buf_zero_count=4258 (buffer empty, i.e. fully sent, in 96.9% of samples) --
    consistent with the ack_pending dominance above.
  cwnd_max=65505, snd_wnd_max=65535 -- window growth proceeded far past the
    8196-byte send buffer; the 8196-byte buffer itself is therefore the plausible
    binding constraint at this cell, consistent with the experiment's framing of
    TCP_SND_BUF as the probed factor. Not independently re-derived/re-argued here --
    flagged only as consistent, not as new proof.

=== Identity cross-check (N13) ===
  run_id in instr_dump JSON: "pilot20_L0_r50_1" (matches instr_reset argument).
  Single TCP connection observed host-side, source port 49153, active window
  [0.0, 88.0]s by iperf's own per-second timestamps.
  Guest accumulator window: ticks [7239, 16028] @ 100 ticks/s = [72.39s, 160.28s]
  since RT-Thread boot -- an absolute offset, not directly comparable to the host's
  run-relative [0.0, 88.0]s, but the SPAN (87.89s) matches the host's span (88.0s)
  to within 0.11s, which is the cross-check that matters here (N13 is about
  confusing which run a connection/sample set belongs to, not about absolute clock
  sync across host/guest -- there is only one connection and one run_id in this
  attempt, so there was no actual ambiguity to resolve, but the convergent span
  arithmetic is recorded as the applied method, per the plan's requirement to use
  port + timestamp window + run_id together rather than a positional convention
  alone).

